  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Home and reports</h1>
  <p class="lede">Home is the short list of what to do next. Reports are the longer picture. Neither one is advice.</p>

  <h2>Home</h2>
  <p>The Home tab leads with spending pace and what is left to assign. Under that is a checklist: assign this month, monthly targets, approve transactions that are waiting, cover overspending, and reconcile accounts that have gone a while without it. Balance outlook says whether scheduled pay, rent, and bills stay within each account.</p>
  <p>A new budget can offer <strong>Explore with Sample Data</strong>. That sample never mixes with your own budget.</p>

  <h2>Reports</h2>
  <p>Open the <strong>Reports</strong> tab. What you can open:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spending</strong> by category for the month.</li>
    <li><strong>Income vs Spending</strong>, including a savings rate. A longer average on that screen is an estimate of pace, not a promise.</li>
    <li><strong>Reimbursements</strong>, for what is still owed to you.</li>
    <li><strong>Net worth</strong> over time, from the accounts you track.</li>
    <li><strong>Sustainable spending</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Your Journey</strong>, the milestones you have met and the ones still ahead.</li>
    <li>Registered-account contribution room, when you have an RRSP, TFSA, or RESP. A withdrawal-tax figure there is an estimate, not tax advice.</li>
    <li>A currency view when the budget has foreign accounts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The numbers are computed on the device that holds the budget.</p>
</article>
